  "query_preview": "..\n\n1018 mb surface high pressure drifts overhead this morning, causing \ncalm winds to organize out of the southwest by early afternoon. For \nnow, weak gradient flow and clear skies has led to early stages of \nradiative fog development. The best signal for this holds over the \nSaginaw Valley/Thumb in closer proximity to lake moisture, but not \nout of the question to see some patchy fog develop further south. \nOtherwise, expecting another sunny and dry fall day with highs in \nthe mid-70s. \n\nIn regards to our late week system, there have been a few notable \ntrends in the 18z/00z guidance. The first is Wednesday, in which \nmodels are trending toward a stronger southern stream low lifting \nalong the eastern CONUS ridge and into northern Lower MI. The \nstronger wave is able to flatten the western flank of the ridge to\nbring forcing/moisture and thus rain chances into the Saginaw \nValley/Thumb regions Wednesday morning. There are still concerns \nabout how well the low levels can top-down saturate given the lag in\nGulf moisture transport, but recent soundings show a shallower layer\nof dry air. NBM PoPs have increased accordingly with 40-60% rain \nchances showing up north of M-59 for Wednesday. Elsewhere, increasing\ncloud cover is expected but greater depth to the dry air should keep\nprecipitation limited Wednesday. \n\nSecond trend has been a more uncertain forecast for Wednesday night\ninto Thursday as Hurricane Polo makes its extratropical transition \nand sends a warm front into the Great Lakes. The trend has been for \nthe bulk of moisture transport to be anchored more closely to the \ndeformation axis, keeping it west of SE Michigan. Disjointed column \nmoisture thus limits confidence in rain chances until the next wave\nof moisture arrives Thursday night. Regardless of moisture \ntransport, the strong SW LLJ will ensure warmer temperatures Thursday\nwith highs in the upper 70s and breezy conditions (20-25 mph gusts).\n\nWhile model divergence increases daytime Thursday, it then converges\nagain as the cold front slides through early Friday morning and \nconnects with the shearing tropical moisture. This will be our best \nchance at heavy rainfall as models hint at frontogenetic enhancement \nto the precipitation axis, even as MUCAPE holds under 500 J/kg. One \nof the other notable trends in the guidance was the phasing of the \nPacific closed low with the northern stream trough, which ultimately \ndelays the clearance of the surface trough to Friday afternoon. This \nkeeps rain chances going through at least the first half of Friday, \nwhich combined with earlier start to precipitation Wednesday boosts \nQPF totals toward an inch or greater by Friday night. This will \nespecially be the case north of M-59, where there has been an \nincrease in the number of 1+ inch ensemble member forecasts. \n\nAggressive drying trend arrives Friday night into Saturday post-\nfront. Assuming saturated ground and full clearing, radiative fog \npotential returns Saturday morning. Encroaching thermal trough will \nbring high temperatures for next weekend into the mid-60s with lows \nin the 40s.",
